= Conway's Game of Life =

'''Conway's Game of Life''' is a two-dimensional binary cellular automaton devised by mathematician John Horton Conway and introduced to a wide audience by Martin Gardner in the October 1970 issue of ''Scientific American''.<ref name="gardner">[https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/mathematical-games-1970-10/ Gardner, M. (1970), “Mathematical Games: The fantastic combinations of John Conway's new solitaire game ‘life’,” ''Scientific American'' 223(4), 120–123.]</ref>

Life evolves on a square lattice of cells. Each cell is either live or dead. All cells update simultaneously from the eight cells in their Moore neighborhood. The rule is commonly written '''B3/S23''': a dead cell is born when exactly three neighbors are live, and a live cell survives when two or three neighbors are live.

Despite its name, Life is a mathematical dynamical system. Its cells are not biological organisms.

== Formal rule ==

Let

<math>x_t(i,j)\in\{0,1\}</math>

be the state of cell <math>(i,j)</math> at generation <math>t</math>.

Let

<math>n_t(i,j)=\sum_{(a,b)\in N(i,j)}x_t(a,b)</math>

be the number of live cells among the eight nearest neighbors.

Then:

<math>x_{t+1}(i,j)=1</math>

iff either:

* <math>x_t(i,j)=0</math> and <math>n_t(i,j)=3</math>; or
* <math>x_t(i,j)=1</math> and <math>n_t(i,j)\in\{2,3\}</math>.

Otherwise the next state is dead.

The update is synchronous.

== Birth and survival notation ==

Life's rulestring is:

<pre>
B3/S23
</pre>

'''B3''' means birth with exactly three live neighbors.

'''S23''' means survival with two or three live neighbors.

Many other Life-like cellular automata use the same notation with different neighbor counts.

== Infinite lattice and simulations ==

The mathematical Game of Life is normally defined on an infinite square lattice.

Computer simulations must use a finite representation.

Possible implementations include:

* a finite rectangle with dead exterior cells;
* periodic wraparound boundaries;
* dynamically expanding sparse storage.

Boundary conditions can alter pattern behavior.

A glider on a torus can eventually wrap around and interact with itself, which does not happen in the same way on an infinite empty lattice.

== Initial configuration ==

The initial set of live cells is the '''seed'''.

The deterministic update rule then determines every later generation.

For a fixed infinite-lattice seed, there is no additional randomness in standard Life.

Different seeds can produce dramatically different long-term behavior.

== Still lifes ==

A '''still life''' is a finite pattern unchanged by one generation of Life.

Examples include:

* block;
* beehive;
* loaf;
* boat.

A 2×2 block is the simplest familiar still life.

Each live cell has enough neighbors to survive and every adjacent dead cell avoids exactly three live neighbors that would cause unwanted births.

== Oscillators ==

An '''oscillator''' returns to its original configuration after a finite period.

The '''blinker''' has period 2.

Three adjacent live cells alternate between horizontal and vertical arrangements.

Other oscillators have much longer periods.

The period is the smallest positive number of generations after which the pattern repeats exactly.

== Spaceships ==

A '''spaceship''' returns to its original shape after a finite number of generations translated by a nonzero displacement.

The '''glider''' is the best-known example.

A glider repeats after four generations shifted one cell diagonally in both coordinates.

Spaceships demonstrate persistent moving structure in a translation-invariant local rule.

== Glider gun ==

A '''gun''' is a finite pattern that periodically emits spaceships while remaining bounded in its core behavior.

Bill Gosper and collaborators discovered the first glider gun in 1970.

This discovery disproved Conway's early conjecture that no finite initial pattern could grow without bound.

A glider gun emits gliders indefinitely, so the total population and occupied region can grow without limit.

== Puffers, rakes, and breeders ==

Life contains several classes of unbounded-growth patterns.

'''Puffers''' move while leaving debris.

'''Rakes''' move while emitting spaceships.

'''Breeders''' construct objects that themselves produce additional growing structures.

These patterns show that unlimited growth can arise from finite seeds.

== Methuselahs ==

A '''methuselah''' is a small initial pattern that evolves for many generations before settling into stable or periodic debris.

The R-pentomino is a famous example.

Methuselahs illustrate how small seeds can generate long transients.

Long transient behavior should not be confused with nonperiodic behavior forever.

== Population and bounding box ==

Useful observables include:

* number of live cells;
* bounding-box dimensions;
* number of connected objects;
* growth rate.

Population alone does not characterize structure.

Two patterns with equal live-cell counts can have completely different dynamics.

== Emergence ==

Life is a canonical example of [[Emergence]].

The update rule specifies only local births and survivals.

Higher-level objects such as gliders, guns, oscillators, and computational circuits are not separate primitives in the rule.

They are recurring configurations of cell states.

This does not make them inexplicable: their behavior can be derived from repeated application of B3/S23.

== Self-organization ==

Some Life patterns form stable or moving structures from less organized seeds.

This motivates discussion of [[Self-organization]].

However, standard Life does not adapt toward a goal and contains no built-in fitness or learning mechanism.

Self-organization should therefore be used descriptively rather than as evidence of agency.

== Computation ==

Life can implement logical operations using streams of gliders and other engineered structures.

Collisions can route, suppress, or generate patterns.

With suitable constructions, these mechanisms can represent logic gates, memory, and universal computation.

Paul Rendell constructed explicit Turing-machine implementations in Life and presents a detailed universality proof.<ref name="rendell">[https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-319-19842-2 Rendell, P. (2016), ''Turing Machine Universality of the Game of Life'', Springer.]</ref>

== Turing completeness ==

[[Turing completeness]] means that, given a suitable encoding and unbounded resources, Life can simulate any computation performable by a Turing machine.

This is an existential statement.

It does not mean that arbitrary Life patterns are useful programs or that Life is computationally efficient.

A universal construction can be enormous and slow relative to conventional hardware.

== Logical universality versus physical universality ==

Universal computation is sometimes implemented through glider streams representing bits or signals.

The Life universe remains governed only by local cell updates.

The computation is encoded in initial conditions and interpreted through higher-level pattern behavior.

This illustrates how universal computation can arise from a simple cellular-automaton rule.

== Undecidability ==

Universal computation implies that some general questions about Life configurations are undecidable.

If arbitrary Turing-machine computations can be encoded in Life, then a general procedure deciding whether every encoded computation eventually halts would solve the halting problem.

Specific Life instances can still be analyzed or simulated.

Undecidability concerns a universal algorithm for all possible cases.

== Garden of Eden configurations ==

A '''Garden of Eden''' pattern is a configuration with no predecessor under the Life rule.

No earlier generation evolves into it.

Such configurations demonstrate that the global update map is not surjective.

The concept is distinct from still life or extinction.

== Finite boards ==

A finite Life board has only finitely many global states.

For <math>N</math> cells, there are

<math>2^N</math>

possible configurations.

A deterministic finite-state system must eventually enter a previously visited state.

Therefore every finite-board trajectory eventually becomes periodic, including the special case of a fixed point.

This result does not imply that the infinite-lattice system is periodic.

== Sparse simulation ==

On a mostly empty infinite plane, storing every dead cell is wasteful.

A sparse implementation can store only live cells.

To compute the next generation, it counts neighbors for live cells and their nearby candidate birth sites.

All cells with nonzero chance of changing must be considered.

== Hashlife ==

'''Hashlife''' is an advanced algorithm for accelerating Life and related cellular automata by memoizing hierarchical regions and skipping repeated evolution.

It can advance highly regular patterns by enormous numbers of generations efficiently.

Its performance depends on repeated spatial and temporal structure.

Hashlife is not uniformly faster for every chaotic or small pattern.

== Verification patterns ==

Several small patterns are useful implementation tests:

'''Block''' — remains unchanged.

'''Blinker''' — period 2.

'''Glider''' — repeats after four generations translated diagonally.

A correct implementation should reproduce these away from boundaries.

These tests also detect the common bug of updating cells in place rather than synchronously.

== In-place update error ==

If changed cells immediately affect later cells during the same generation, the simulation no longer implements standard Life.

Correct computation conceptually uses:

<pre>
next = rule(current)
current = next
</pre>

or an equivalent method preserving simultaneous state transition.

== Pattern catalogs ==

A large research and hobbyist community has cataloged enormous numbers of Life patterns, oscillators, spaceships, guns, logical constructions, and growth phenomena.

Catalog evidence is valuable for known constructions.

Claims about mathematical universality or undecidability should still be grounded in formal arguments rather than pattern popularity.

== Life-like cellular automata ==

Life belongs to the family of outer-totalistic two-state cellular automata on the Moore neighborhood.

Other rules can be written in B/S notation.

Small rule changes can produce qualitatively different dynamics.

Therefore observations about B3/S23 should not automatically be generalized to all cellular automata.

== Biological analogy ==

Gardner's original presentation emphasized analogies to birth, death, and populations.<ref name="gardner" />

The analogy is useful pedagogically.

Life cells lack:

* metabolism;
* biochemical heredity;
* cells in the biological sense;
* evolution by natural selection in the base rule;
* subjective experience.

Life is not evidence that biological life can be reduced to B3/S23.

== Artificial life ==

Life influenced [[Artificial life]] because it demonstrates complex, persistent, and computational structures in an artificial rule-based world.

Artificial-life researchers use cellular automata among many other models.

Life is one historically important substrate, not a complete theory of living systems.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Conway's Game of Life establishes that a deterministic local rule can support rich emergent structure and universal computation.

It does not establish that complexity, computation, biological life, intelligence, or consciousness are equivalent.

Each stronger claim requires separate definitions and evidence.

